{
    "background_story": "Marigold was a peculiar strain of bacteria that had developed a unique resistance to the antibiotic tobramycin. In a lab, scientists had been studying her remarkable ability to adapt and thrive in environments where tobramycin was present. As they delved deeper into Marigold's genetic makeup, they discovered a novel enzyme that allowed her to break down the antibiotic's molecular structure. This breakthrough led to a new understanding of bacterial resistance and the development of more effective treatments. Marigold's resilience in the face of tobramycin had inadvertently paved the way for significant advancements in the field of microbiology, earning her a special place in the annals of scientific discovery.",
